1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:01,220
Welcome back to the Deep Dive.

2
00:00:01,980 --> 00:00:07,470
So, um, today we're looking at a pretty massive headache for government digital systems.

3
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:14,780
We are exploring why these systems remain so fragmented even when there are, you know, strict national strategies in place.

4
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:19,810
Yeah, it's a huge issue because even when procurement rules mandate things like open APIs,

5
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:20,560
uh,

6
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:24,870
the citizen still ends up filling out the exact same form five times.

7
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:26,080
Right. Exactly.

8
00:00:26,680 --> 00:00:31,380
And if you are reviewing new technology deployments in your ministry, you see this constantly.

9
00:00:31,590 --> 00:00:40,510
You might ask, why does a newly funded program inside your ministry still build its own, uh, isolated payments or identity system instead of just sharing?

10
00:00:40,780 --> 00:00:45,120
Well, the standard assumption is that these project teams just lack, you know, technical discipline.

11
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:47,080
Yeah. But that is actually not the case at all.

12
00:00:47,160 --> 00:00:51,040
Oh, really? Yeah. Not at all. The reality is that project teams are highly rational.

13
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:53,360
They do exactly what they are funded to do.

14
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:54,780
Which is what? Just get it done.

15
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:57,500
Exactly. They need to ship on time and on budget.

16
00:00:57,840 --> 00:01:02,930
But wait, wouldn't reusing another ministry's existing system be, like, way easier and cheaper for them?

17
00:01:03,380 --> 00:01:04,180
You would think so, right.

18
00:01:04,860 --> 00:01:10,480
But reusing means they have to learn the other system, negotiate access, and, uh,

19
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:14,000
basically accept another ministry's delays.

20
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:16,400
Oh, I see. So they just take the path of least resistance.

21
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:21,460
Yeah. Building custom system or a new register is simply faster for that one specific project.

22
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:24,700
Procurement rules can require certain behavior, sure,

23
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:30,340
but they cannot magically make interoperability the cheapest choice for an isolated project team.

24
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:30,760
Right.

25
00:01:31,480 --> 00:01:32,410
So, um,

26
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:35,580
how do we actually change the incentives then?

27
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:38,560
How do we make reuse the logical choice?

28
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:42,140
The math only really changes when you look across the entire digital portfolio.

29
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:43,260
Okay. Walk me through that.

30
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:45,140
Sure. So let's walk through four ministries.

31
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:48,180
The first ministry pays to build a robust identity system.

32
00:01:48,310 --> 00:01:50,520
Got it. Then the second ministry does not build.

33
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:51,580
It consumes the first one.

34
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:52,360
Okay. Makes sense.

35
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:54,600
The third ministry does not build, it consumes.

36
00:01:54,620 --> 00:01:57,000
The fourth ministry does not build, it consumes.

37
00:01:57,040 --> 00:01:59,120
Wow. Okay. And over time?

38
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:05,240
Over a five-year period, this coordinated model saves a meaningful share of its sectoral digital spend.

39
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:09,140
But, you know, this view requires a national enterprise architecture.

40
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:11,360
Right, because you have to see the big picture.

41
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:17,840
So what other blind spots exist when projects just operate in these silos?

42
00:02:18,290 --> 00:02:22,860
Well, there are really two critical things that isolated projects just cannot deliver.

43
00:02:22,900 --> 00:02:25,360
The first is sustainability.

44
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:27,519
What do you mean by sustainability in this context?

45
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:33,640
Think about it twelve years from now, the original vendor is gone, the underlying open source libraries have forked,

46
00:02:34,360 --> 00:02:36,980
and, uh, no one actually understands the system anymore.

47
00:02:37,260 --> 00:02:41,760
Oh, wow. Yeah, because they're incentivized to ship today, not plan for a decade later.

48
00:02:41,820 --> 00:02:44,240
Exactly. And the second thing is complexity reduction.

49
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:49,230
Right. Right. To get launch approval, project teams basically say yes to everyone to please stakeholders.

50
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:53,660
Five years later, the system is just way too complex to even maintain safely.

51
00:02:53,670 --> 00:02:56,300
That sounds like a nightmare for whoever inherits it.

52
00:02:56,340 --> 00:02:59,220
So how can your program avoid repeating these exact mistakes?

53
00:02:59,450 --> 00:03:01,900
This is where the PAERA framework comes in.

54
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:06,829
Oh. PAERA. It stands for the Public Administration Ecosystem Reference Architecture.

55
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:10,200
PAERA. Okay, what does that actually do for a ministry?

56
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:18,560
It documents twenty years of international lessons on which architectural decisions actually survive technology changes over time.

57
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:19,980
Oh, that's huge.

58
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:26,620
Yeah. It details what actually works. Applying this framework ensures your vendor builds on proven structural foundations.

59
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:27,890
So you don't have to, like,

60
00:03:28,820 --> 00:03:31,780
finance the discovery of those lessons all over again the hard way.

61
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:34,780
Exactly. It removes all that unnecessary risk.

62
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:44,320
Well, to summarize, an enterprise architecture provides the essential planning function, which is why your minister must commission it deliberately with sustained funding.

63
00:03:44,540 --> 00:03:46,420
Absolutely. It cannot just be an afterthought.

64
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:46,780
Right.

65
00:03:47,420 --> 00:03:49,480
Well, I want to leave you with a final thought to mull over.

66
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:54,640
If an enterprise architecture is this vital for today's basic interoperability,

67
00:03:55,280 --> 00:04:02,430
consider how much more critical this whole of government planning will be to survive the rapid technological shifts we're going to see over the next decade.

68
00:04:03,140 --> 00:04:05,080
Definitely something you will need to prepare for.
